'\" t
.\" blas-microbenchmark(1). make turns this .in into blas-microbenchmark.1,
.\" then into the site's blas-microbenchmark.html; edit the .in.
.\" man/test_man_*.sh check it against the programs: see
.\" doc/dev/documentation.md.
.TH BLAS-MICROBENCHMARK 1 2026-10-05 "blas-microbenchmark @PACKAGE_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.\" No hyphenation, no justification: both hurt in a terminal, and
.\" hyphenation splits literals such as "# label:".
.nh
.ds AD l
.ad l
.SH NAME
blas-microbenchmark, bmb_dasum, bmb_daxpy, bmb_dcopy, bmb_ddot, bmb_dnrm2,
bmb_dscal, bmb_dswap, bmb_dgemv, bmb_dger, bmb_dsymv, bmb_dsyr, bmb_dsyr2,
bmb_dtrmv, bmb_dtrsv, bmb_dgemm, bmb_dsymm, bmb_dsyrk, bmb_dsyr2k, bmb_dtrmm,
bmb_dtrsm \- microbenchmarks for BLAS routines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bmb_\c
.I routine
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each
.BI bmb_ routine
times one double-precision BLAS routine, called through CBLAS, over a sweep
of sizes and thread counts, and prints one row per point: the time of a
call and the rate it achieved.
All of them take the same options.
They run against the BLAS library chosen when the package was built
(OpenBLAS, BLIS, Netlib reference BLAS, NVPL or ArmPL), or, built
against FlexiBLAS, the one FlexiBLAS loads when they start
.RB ( FLEXIBLAS ,
see
.BR ENVIRONMENT );
.B \-\-version
says which.
.PP
To compare libraries, build the package once against each and run the
same command with each build, or build it once against FlexiBLAS and run
the command under each library; then turn the results into charts with
.BR bmb_report (1).
.SS Routines
.TS
l l.
Level 1 (vector)	dasum daxpy dcopy ddot dnrm2 dscal dswap
Level 2 (matrix\-vector)	dgemv dger dsymv dsyr dsyr2 dtrmv dtrsv
Level 3 (matrix\-matrix)	dgemm dsymm dsyrk dsyr2k dtrmm dtrsm
.TE
.PP
Level 1 routines are sized with
.BR \-v .
Level 2 and 3 routines are sized with
.B \-m
and, for those with two dimensions,
.BR \-M .
Which dimension is which:
.PP
.TS
l l.
dgemv dger dsymm dtrmm dtrsm	\-m is M, \-M is N
dgemm	\-m is M and K, \-M is N
dsyrk dsyr2k	\-m is N, \-M is K
dsymv dsyr dsyr2 dtrmv dtrsv	\-m is N (square; no \-M)
.TE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-x, \-\-warmup " n
Untimed calls before measuring each point.
Default: 1.
.TP
.BI "\-i, \-\-iterations " n
Timed samples taken at each point.
Default: 10.
.TP
.BI "\-b, \-\-batch " n
Calls averaged per sample;
0 chooses automatically, per point (see
.BR "HOW A NUMBER IS MEASURED" ).
.B \-\-batch 1
times every call on its own.
dtrmv, dtrsv, dtrmm and dtrsm are always timed that way: their operand
is restored before every call, and a larger
.B \-b
is ignored with a warning.
Default: 0.
.TP
.BI "\-v, \-\-vector\-size " sweep
Vector sizes, for level 1 routines.
Default: 4096.
.TP
.BI "\-m, \-\-matrix\-dim1 " sweep
First matrix dimension, for level 2 and 3 routines.
Default: 4096.
.TP
.BI "\-M, \-\-matrix\-dim2 " sweep
Second matrix dimension.
Without it, matrices are square: the second dimension follows the first at
every point.
With it, every combination of the two is measured.
.TP
.BI "\-t, \-\-thread\-count " sweep
Number of BLAS threads.
Default: 1.
See
.BR THREADS .
.TP
.B "\-s, \-\-statistics"
Also report, per point, the mean of the samples, their standard deviation
(over the samples as measured, divided by
.I n
rather than
.IR n \-1),
the slowest sample, the batch size and the median of the samples, as
columns in that order after the rates.
.TP
.B "\-c, \-\-verify"
Before timing each point, check that the library computes the right
result there; stop with exit status 2 if it does not.
See
.BR "CHECKING RESULTS" .
Default: on.
.TP
.B "\-C, \-\-no\-verify"
Do not check the results.
.TP
.BI "\-L, \-\-layout " order
How the matrices are stored and passed to CBLAS:
.B row
(row-major, as C stores arrays) or
.B col
(column-major, as Fortran and LAPACK do).
A library may handle the two differently: CBLAS row-major calls are
often turned into column-major ones on the transposed problem, so a
Fortran program can see other numbers than the default does.
The problem is the same either way (the same sizes, the upper triangle,
no transpose); only its storage changes.
Ignored, with a warning, by the vector routines.
Default: row.
.TP
.BI "\-l, \-\-label " text
Tag the results, for what no automatic field records: a BIOS setting, a
NUMA binding, SMT off.
1 to 200 characters, no control characters.
Default: none, and no label is written.
.TP
.BI "\-o, \-\-output " file
Also save the results to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI "\-f, \-\-output\-format " format
.B csv
or
.B json
for
.BR \-o .
Default: guessed from the extension of the
.B \-o
file, otherwise csv.
.TP
.B "\-h, \-\-help"
Show a summary of the options.
.TP
.B "\-V, \-\-version"
Show the version, the BLAS library and the machine, without running
anything.
.SS Sweeps
The four size options each take a
.IR sweep :
.PP
.TS
l l l.
\fImax\fP	that one size	4096
\fImin\fP:\fImax\fP	doubling	256:4096 \(-> 256 512 1024 2048 4096
\fImin\fP:\fImax\fP:\fIstep\fP	linear	1000:4000:1000 \(-> 1000 2000 3000 4000
\fIv1\fP,\fIv2\fP,...	exactly those	64,1000,4096
.TE
.PP
.I max
is always measured, even when the step would overshoot it:
100:1000:300 gives 100, 400, 700 and 1000.
A sweep has at most 256 points.
A size whose memory would not fit in
.B size_t
is refused before anything is allocated.
.SH HOW A NUMBER IS MEASURED
Reading the clock costs tens of nanoseconds, which is the whole duration of
a small level 1 call.
So each
.I sample
times a
.I batch
of calls and divides by the batch size.
The batch size is chosen per point, so that a batch lasts long enough for
the clock not to matter;
calls that already take longer get a batch of 1, so nothing changes for
level 3 or for large sizes.
.B \-s
shows the batch size chosen.
.PP
The reported time is the
.B fastest
sample, not the mean.
A sample is already an average over a whole batch, so the fastest is not a
lucky call: it is the batch that ran with the least interference.
One scheduling hiccup inflates a sample a great deal, and would drag a
mean with it;
.B \-s
shows the mean and the spread for that reason.
.PP
Two rates are derived from that time:
.TP
.B GFLOP/s
the conventional operation count of the routine (2\(muM\(muN\(muK for
dgemm, K\(muN\(mu(N+1) for dsyrk, ...) divided by the time.
Not shown for dcopy and dswap, which do no arithmetic.
.TP
.B GB/s
the bytes the routine must move \(em each operand read once, each result
written once, counted the way STREAM counts them \(em divided by the time.
Levels 1 and 2 only: level 3 routines reuse their operands from cache, so
a rate built from that traffic would not measure bandwidth.
.SH CHECKING RESULTS
A benchmark times a call whatever it computes: a library that gets the
result wrong \(em a broken build, a bug at one size or thread count \(em
can look fast.
So each point is first checked, unless
.B \-C
is given: one call on the operands about to be timed,
at that size and thread count, compared with a reference computed
independently.
.PP
Level 1 and 2 results are recomputed directly, which costs about one
more call.
Level 3 results are checked by random projection: for a random vector
.IR r ,
.I C*r
is compared with
.IR A*(B*r) ,
and for dtrsm the residual
.I U*(X*r)
with
.IR B*r .
That takes as long as a level 2 call, where recomputing
.I C
would take as long as the call itself, and a wrong
.I C
gives a wrong
.I C*r
for almost every
.IR r .
Routines that update one triangle are also checked to leave the other
as it was.
.PP
The tolerance is a bound on rounding error, proportional to the number
of terms in each element and to their magnitudes: a correct library is
well inside it, a wrong one far outside.
A wrong result is reported with the routine, the size, the thread count
and the element that differs; nothing more is measured, and no
.B \-o
file is written.
The check needs memory for a few vectors the size of the result, and
for daxpy, dswap, dgemv and dsymv a copy of the vector they write.
.PP
The check is on by default, as it is in STREAM and HPL: a number from a
library that computes the wrong thing is worth less than none.
.B \-C
turns it off, for a library already checked, or where that memory
matters.
.SH THREADS
.B \-t
sets the number of threads through the library's own interface.
If an environment variable that the library reads says otherwise,
.B \-t
wins, and a warning says so:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 bmb_dgemm \-t 1
OMP_NUM_THREADS is ignored! Set to 4 but option \-t is set to 1.
.EE
.RE
.PP
Without
.BR \-t ,
those variables apply as usual: the first one set gives the thread count.
A value that is not a whole number of threads, from 1 to 2147483647
(0, \-2, 2,1, or one with a blank in it), is ignored with a warning, and
the next variable is read.
Netlib reference BLAS is single-threaded, and
.B \-t
has no effect with it.
.PP
Nothing here sets thread affinity, and operands are allocated and filled by
the main thread, so on a machine with several NUMA nodes every page lands
on that thread's node.
Both matter as soon as more than one thread runs; see
.BR EXAMPLES .
.SH HARDWARE COUNTERS
GFLOP/s and GB/s say how fast, not why.
Hardware counters \(em instructions, cycles, cache misses, the
floating-point operations the CPU actually executed \(em say why, and the
benchmarks leave reading them to the tools made for it,
.BR perf (1)
and
.BR likwid\-perfctr ,
run around them.
Both count the whole process: allocating and filling the operands, the
.B \-\-verify
check, the batch calibration and the warm-up, not only the timed calls.
.PP
Two runs that differ only in
.B \-i
take that away.
With
.B \-b 1
each sample is one call, preceded by the operand restore of the routines
that need one, so the difference between the two counts, divided by the
difference in
.BR \-i ,
is what one call costs:
.PP
.RS
.EX
perf stat \-e instructions,cycles \-\- bmb_dgemm \-x 0 \-b 1 \-i 10 \-m 1024
perf stat \-e instructions,cycles \-\- bmb_dgemm \-x 0 \-b 1 \-i 110 \-m 1024
.EE
.RE
.PP
Here, one call is a hundredth of the difference.
Keep to one size and one thread count per pair of runs, and to sizes
where a call takes long enough that the clock read around it does not
count.
.PP
The floating-point events have CPU-specific names, which
.B perf list
gives: for instance
.B fp_arith_inst_retired.256b_packed_double
on recent Intel CPUs, or
.B fp_ret_sse_avx_ops.all
on AMD Zen.
.B likwid\-perfctr
groups them per CPU, under names most CPUs share
.RB ( FLOPS_DP ,
.BR L3 ;
.B likwid\-perfctr \-a
lists the groups of the one at hand), and pins the threads to the cores
it measures:
.PP
.RS
.EX
likwid\-perfctr \-C 0\-3 \-g FLOPS_DP bmb_dgemm \-x 0 \-b 1 \-i 10 \-t 4 \-m 4096
.EE
.RE
.PP
Most virtual machines, containers and WSL expose no hardware counters,
and
.B perf
then reports them as not supported.
On a real machine, reading them may need
.I /proc/sys/kernel/perf_event_paranoid
at 2 or lower for
.BR perf ,
or the access daemon or root for
.BR likwid\-perfctr .
.SH OUTPUT
Results go to standard output, one row per point, as they are measured;
warnings and errors go to standard error.
Every result starts with where it came from:
.PP
.RS
.EX
# blas\-microbenchmark @PACKAGE_VERSION@
# backend: openblas (OpenBLAS 0.3.26 DYNAMIC_ARCH Haswell MAX_THREADS=64)
# verified: every point, against a reference, before it was timed
# layout: row\-major
# cpu: Intel(R) Core(TM) Ultra 7 155U (14 logical CPUs, 1 NUMA node)
# caches: L1d 48K, L1i 64K, L2 2M, L3 12M
# frequency: governor performance, turbo off
# os: Linux 6.6.87 x86_64
# date: 2026\-10\-02T09:05:04Z
# routine: dgemm
.EE
.RE
.PP
The library's version string, where it exposes one, tells apart two
builds of the same library.
The caches show where a sweep leaves each level.
The frequency line gives the CPU frequency governor (several, joined by
.BR / ,
when CPUs differ) and whether turbo is on, as Linux exposes them in
.IR /sys ;
virtual machines and containers often expose neither.
When either lets the frequency change during the run, a governor other
than
.B performance
or turbo on, a warning on standard error says so and gives the commands
that fix it; the run goes on.
A field that cannot be read on a machine is left out, never guessed.
The hostname is deliberately not recorded.
With
.BR \-l ,
a
.B "#\ label:"
line follows the backend, and unless
.B \-C
was given, a
.B "#\ verified:"
line comes next, then, for the routines with a matrix, the
.B "#\ layout:"
.RB ( \-L ).
.PP
With
.BR \-o ,
the results are also saved to a file:
.TP
.B csv
the same comment lines, then a header and one row per point.
Readers must skip lines starting with
.BR # ,
e.g.\&
.B pd.read_csv(file, comment=\(dq#\(dq)
in pandas.
.TP
.B json
one object holding the same information as fields:
.BR version ,
.BR backend ,
.BR blas ,
.BR label ,
.BR verified ,
.B layout
.RB ( row " or " col ,
for the routines with a matrix),
.BR date ;
.B machine
with
.BR cpu ,
.BR logical_cpus ,
.BR numa_nodes ,
.BR caches ,
.BR governor ,
.B turbo
(true or false)
and
.BR os ;
.B routine
and, naming its dimensions,
.B dim1_label
and
.BR dim2_label .
Then a
.B results
array, one object per point:
.BR thread_count ,
.BR dim1 ,
.BR dim2 ,
.BR time_s ,
.BR gflops ,
.BR gbytes_per_s ,
and with
.BR \-s ,
.BR mean_s ,
.BR stddev_s ,
.BR max_s ,
.B calls_per_sample
and
.BR median_s .
A field that does not apply is left out.
This is what
.BR bmb_report (1)
reads.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when every point was measured and every result written;
1 for an invalid option or size, or when standard output or the
.B \-o
file could not be written \(em a table cut short by a full disk must not
pass for a complete one;
2 when a result was wrong (see
.BR "CHECKING RESULTS" ),
in which case nothing is written to the
.B \-o
file.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The thread-count variables the library reads, in the order it reads them:
.TP
OpenBLAS
.BR OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS ,
.BR GOTO_NUM_THREADS ,
.B OMP_NUM_THREADS
.TP
BLIS
.BR BLIS_NUM_THREADS ,
.B OMP_NUM_THREADS
.TP
NVPL, ArmPL
.B OMP_NUM_THREADS
.TP
FlexiBLAS
those of the library it loaded, as above; none for Netlib.
.PP
.B FLEXIBLAS
picks the library a build against FlexiBLAS loads, by the name
.B flexiblas list
gives it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
FLEXIBLAS=OPENBLAS\-OPENMP bmb_dgemm \-m 512:2048 \-o openblas.json
FLEXIBLAS=BLIS\-OPENMP bmb_dgemm \-m 512:2048 \-o blis.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
The results name the library loaded: backend
.BR flexiblas/openblas\-openmp ,
and FlexiBLAS's version with the library's name.
.PP
.BR OMP_PROC_BIND " and " OMP_PLACES
pin the threads of the OpenMP-threaded libraries.
.PP
.BR NO_COLOR ,
when set and not empty, turns off the colours of warnings and errors,
which are only used on a terminal anyway.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @pkglibexecdir@/level1
.TQ
.I @pkglibexecdir@/level2
.TQ
.I @pkglibexecdir@/level3
The benchmarks, grouped by BLAS level rather than put on the
.BR PATH .
To run them by name:
.RS
.PP
.EX
BMB=@pkglibexecdir@
export PATH=\(dq$BMB/level1:$BMB/level2:$BMB/level3:$PATH\(dq
.EE
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Sweep ddot from 1K to 16M elements, with the statistics, saved as CSV:
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_ddot \-v 1024:16777216 \-s \-o ddot.csv
.EE
.RE
.PP
Every point is checked before it is timed. For a library already
checked, skip that:
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_dgemm \-m 512:4096 \-t 1:8 \-C \-o dgemm.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
The sizes that matter to you, and nothing else:
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_dgemm \-m 1024,2048,4096
.EE
.RE
.PP
Square matrices, then every shape (the grid
.BR bmb_report (1)
draws as a heatmap):
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_dgemm \-m 512:2048                 # 512\(S2, 1024\(S2, 2048\(S2
bmb_dgemm \-m 512:2048 \-M 512:2048     # all 9 combinations
.EE
.RE
.PP
Thread scaling, threads pinned \(em OMP_* for the OpenMP-threaded
libraries, taskset for a pthread-built OpenBLAS:
.PP
.RS
.EX
OMP_PROC_BIND=close OMP_PLACES=cores bmb_dgemm \-t 1:8 \-o dgemm.json
taskset \-c 0\-7 bmb_dgemm \-t 1:8 \-o dgemm.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
On a machine with several NUMA nodes, spread the pages over all of them,
or measure one node at a time \(em usually what you want to compare:
.PP
.RS
.EX
numactl \-\-interleave=all bmb_dgemm \-t 1:32
numactl \-\-cpunodebind=0 \-\-membind=0 bmb_dgemm \-t 1:16
.EE
.RE
.PP
Two conditions on the same machine, told apart in the report by their
labels (turbo is recorded, but a series is not split by it):
.PP
.RS
.EX
bmb_dgemm \-m 1024:8192 \-l \(dqturbo on\(dq  \-o turbo\-on.json
bmb_dgemm \-m 1024:8192 \-l \(dqturbo off\(dq \-o turbo\-off.json
bmb_report turbo\-*.json > report.html
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bmb_report (1),
.BR numactl (8),
.BR taskset (1)
.PP
https://github.com/MathieuCAISSA/blas\-microbenchmark
